    "result_text": "The full suite is a single acceptance test class (the project's only test), and it passes with **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same firstName and lastName, case-insensitively \u2014 as a persisted, computed field returned on owner reads.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a `namesakeCount` read-only `int32` field to the `Owner` response schema (alongside `customerCode`/`householdId`). The DTOs are generated from this spec, and MapStruct auto-maps the property to the entity.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** (entity) \u2014 Added the `namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 Compute the count from the existing owners *before* saving the new one and store it on the owner:\n   ```java\n   owner.setNamesakeCount(countNamesakes(owner.getFirstName(), owner.getLastName()));\n   ```\n   The new `countNamesakes` helper counts existing owners whose first and last names both match case-insensitively (`equalsIgnoreCase`). Because it's stored, the value is a stable snapshot echoed back on later `GET`s.\n\n4. **DB schemas** \u2014 Added a `namesake_count` column to the `owners` table in hsqldb, h2, mysql, and postgres schema files, and appended the trailing `NULL` to the positional owner seed inserts in `hsqldb/data.sql` (h2 and postgres seeds use named-column inserts and needed no change).\n\nThe test creates two owners sharing firstName+lastName (with different addresses so the household-duplicate rule doesn't 409), and asserts the second owner's `namesakeCount` is `1` \u2014 which now passes.",
